  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1142 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nWe'll be under the influence of a broad upper level low \ncentered over central Canada today, with a trough passing overhead \nlate today into the overnight hours, with a second subtle weak \nshortwave following close behind during the early morning hours. A \nbackdoor front will be dropping south across the area through the \ncourse of the day. 1/2km winds ramp up to about 25 to 30kts as 850mb \ntemperatures drop from about +2C to around -5 to -8C by Sunday \nmorning. This will help enhance winds by steepening low level \nlapse rates, with the RRFS mixed winds likewise between 25 and \n35kts... fairly in line with NBM winds. The steep lapse rates \nalso results in shallow instability. During the majority of the \nday, with the colder air to the north, the low level instability\nonly results in shallow cumulus, with RRFS BUFKIT profiles \nindicating cloud development between a 4kft to 7kft layer, \nsituated below the dendritic growth zone, with the RAP just\nhaving shallow cumulus less than 1kft thick. Through the late \nafternoon/evening, as we get into the colder air, this layer \ndeepens and we start seeing thicknesses deep enough and into the\ndendritic growth zone suitable for a mention of showers. The \nHRRR likewise generates precipitation under this regime. As \nshould be expected the NBM probables for measuring are too low \nto register due to the convective nature of QPF, however there \nis far more than enough evidence for increasing POPs over NBM to\n20-30%. \n\nEven during the day Sunday, profiles indicate enough low level \ninstability to result in daytime driven cumulus, through by this \npoint drier air aloft and an inversion could limit vertical \ndevelopment. HRRR likewise fails to indicate any isolated/scattered \nreturns so not anticipating any daytime driven showers Sunday.  \n\nHere we're going to step briefly back to Saturday. There is the \ninitial wave which supports precipitation in Nebraska and far southern \nSouth Dakota, probability of precipitation in excess of 0.01\" north \nof the White River is now down to less than 20%. \n\nAn upper ridge builds out west for the start of the work week, with \nnorthwest flow aloft Monday. A weak trough passes under this weak \nridge into the Central Plains, which supports a weak low across \nSouth Dakota. Not much relevant with this feature other than \ndisrupting the southerly return flow off Canadian high pressure. For \nthe latter half of the work week, the pattern develops into a more \ntraditional El-Nino'ish split flow with an upper low across the 4-\nCorners into the Southern Plains with a weak trough rapidly \ncrossing the Northern Plains Wednesday night. Thus, the colder \nair from this weekend is the coolest we'll be looking at, as \nmilder air returns for the work week with nothing to provide an \nimpetus to draw colder air back into the region.",
